From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Douglas Panhota <douglaspands@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 19:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] drm/bridge: dw-mipi-dsi: keep the clock lane in LP for LP commands

The host requests the high speed clock (PHY_TXREQUESTCLKHS) as soon as it
switches to command mode, so a panel initialised from prepare gets its
whole init sequence with the clock lane running in HS. The Rockchip vendor
kernel (4.4 BSP, drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/dw-mipi-dsi.c) does the
opposite: each low power message clears PHY_TXREQUESTCLKHS, and the clock
only starts in enable, after the panel prepare, right before video mode.

On the R36S V5 (ST7703 family panel, init sequence in LP) everything else
of the link now matches the factory kernel register by register, and the
panel still never shows the video. In command mode, when the peripheral
takes its commands in low power (MIPI_DSI_MODE_LPM), leave the clock lane
in LP; video mode and HS commands keep requesting the clock as before.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-mipi-dsi.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-mipi-dsi.c
index 3c1fda9..c8e2156 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-mipi-dsi.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-mipi-dsi.c
@@ -666,7 +666,17 @@ static void dw_mipi_dsi_set_mode(struct dw_mipi_dsi *dsi,
 		dsi_write(dsi, DSI_MODE_CFG, ENABLE_CMD_MODE);
 	}

-	val = PHY_TXREQUESTCLKHS;
+	/*
+	 * In command mode a peripheral that takes its commands in low power
+	 * (MIPI_DSI_MODE_LPM) needs no high speed clock: keep the clock lane in LP
+	 * until video starts, as the Rockchip vendor kernel does, so the init
+	 * sequence of a panel initialised from prepare is sent with the clock lane
+	 * at rest (R36S V5, ST7703 family).
+	 */
+	if (!(mode_flags & MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO) && (dsi->mode_flags & MIPI_DSI_MODE_LPM))
+		val = 0;
+	else
+		val = PHY_TXREQUESTCLKHS;
 	if (dsi->mode_flags & MIPI_DSI_CLOCK_NON_CONTINUOUS)
 		val |= AUTO_CLKLANE_CTRL;
 	dsi_write(dsi, DSI_LPCLK_CTRL, val);
